.TH ELITE_RINGBUFFER 3 "2026-09-24" "ELITEIPC 1.1.0" "Library Functions"
.SH NAME
elite_ringbuffer \- C11 zero-copy SPSC and NCQ-SC64 shared-memory IPC
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <elite_api.h>
/* C11 shared-layout inspection, only when required: */
.B #include <elite_ringbuffer.h>

elite_result elite_platform_admit(void);
elite_result elite_write_reserve(elite_connection *c,
    elite_lease *lease, elite_write_span *span);
elite_result elite_write_commit(elite_connection *c,
    const elite_lease *lease, uint32_t length,
    uint32_t message_type, uint64_t message_id);
elite_result elite_write_abort(elite_connection *c,
    const elite_lease *lease);
elite_result elite_read_borrow(elite_connection *c,
    elite_lease *lease, elite_read_span *span);
elite_result elite_read_release(elite_connection *c,
    const elite_lease *lease);

/* Link through the imported CMake target elite::ringbuffer,
 * or -lelite_ringbuffer with the platform thread dependencies. */
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
ELITEIPC transports CPU payloads between trusted, registered processes using
POSIX shared-memory objects and C11 atomics. It provides fixed-capacity SPSC
and publish-first NCQ-SC64 work-sharing queues. Payload addresses are local
borrowed pointers into mapped storage; they are not serialized pointers.
.PP
The library release is 1.1.0. The shared wire ABI is LE128-V1, numeric
0x00010000. These are different version domains. Use matching native and
Python wrapper/exporter releases; do not replace a loaded library in place.
.SH PLATFORM ADMISSION
Native little-endian x86-64 or ARM64, coherent normal CPU memory, compatible
address-independent lock-free 32- and 64-bit atomics, and the compiled C11
layout are required. The OS page size must be a power of two between 128
and 16384 and divide the 16384-byte format quantum. No 128-bit atomic is
required. Layout declarations use explicit 128-byte isolation cells, not
packed structs. C++ callers use elite_api.h, not an independent std::atomic
overlay on the wire format.
.PP
Linux and macOS are the established target families. The native Darwin
waiting adapter requires macOS 14.4 or later. The Turn 16 FreeBSD port is
explicitly experimental, enabled only with ELITE_EXPERIMENTAL_FREEBSD;
only POLL_ONLY is admitted there. It has no implemented parkable adapter.
Native FreeBSD target execution is a separate qualification gate.
.SH CREATION AND ATTACHMENT
The application owns one serialized elite_authority. Create it with
.B elite_authority_create
using nonzero, nonreused host/authority identities and a maximum backing
size. Fill an elite_config and an array of elite_endpoint_definition records.
.B elite_create
constructs a fresh exclusively named object; it never truncates or resets
an existing live object. Activate it with
.BR elite_object_activate .
.PP
Before exposing a one-shot endpoint grant, register self or an unreaped,
owned child with
.BR elite_object_register_process .
Issue the grant using
.BR elite_object_grant .
The child calls
.B elite_attach
only on that trusted, constructed-object grant. Use a controlled bootstrap
channel. Native grant structs are process-local ABI objects, not a portable
or authenticated serialization format. The bundled demo uses the same
executable on both sides of its private pipe.
.PP
Construction must precede exposure. A correct CRC or a name discovered in
a namespace does not establish this condition. Unregistered descendants
or mapping transfers violate the holder-accounting contract.
.PP
Create or attach errors can leave a non-NULL object or connection carrying
cleanup responsibility. Examine output handles even on failure. Do not
convert an ambiguous OS close result into an unaccounted retry on a reused
file descriptor.
.SH ZERO-COPY WRITE LEASE
.B elite_write_reserve
returns one writable span of at most the configured capacity and an opaque
process-local lease. Write directly to this span. Only one operation/lease
may be outstanding on each endpoint. Do not reuse an active lease output
object for a second reserve.
.PP
Finish all construction and all writable aliases before
.BR elite_write_commit .
Only the valid prefix specified by length is published. The caller supplies
message_type and message_id; neither imposes a total order on producers or
external side effects. Copying an existing buffer into the span is still a
copy; the transport does not conceal it under the zero-copy label.
.PP
After the publication linearization point the producer must not access
mutable descriptor or payload bytes, even if commit has not returned.
.B elite_write_abort
cancels only a still-unpublished write after its aliases end. It cannot
undo publication. Abort consumes the relevant lease epoch; epochs do not
reset when a reservation is cancelled.
.SH READ LEASE AND ALIASES
.B elite_read_borrow
claims one ready record and returns a read-only span with valid length,
type, ID and epoch. Never dereference an observed queue index before winning
ownership. Reading outside the valid prefix is not part of this record.
.PP
End every direct or exported alias before
.BR elite_read_release .
A release enables reuse, not merely removal from the ready queue. A slow
reader retains capacity. A compiler fence, checksum or later generation
check cannot make use through a released pointer safe.
.PP
.B elite_view_retain
and
.B elite_view_end
track wrapper-managed aliases and prevent premature transfer or detach.
They cannot discover arbitrary C pointer copies. The optional Python
exporter retains both mapping and slot lease for memoryview slices/casts;
Python native-call serialization does not serialize arbitrary simultaneous
access through those views. Main-interpreter, GIL-enabled CPython is the
admitted wrapper runtime.
.SH RESULT AND ERROR CONTRACT
Each elite_result contains status, outcome, os_error and a reserved word.
Functions do not encode every failure solely in errno. Inspect status and
outcome together. os_error is meaningful for the particular OS failure.
An OS notification error with outcome PUBLISHED is not an unsent message.
Do not retry publication merely because status is nonzero.
.SS Status codes
.TP
.B ELITE_OK (0)
Operation completed under its stated contract; inspect its outcome.
.TP
.B ELITE_NO_DATA_OBSERVED (1)
No ready data authorized by the current try observation. Not an exact
wall-clock size query.
.TP
.B ELITE_NO_CAPACITY_OBSERVED (2)
No available storage observed. Held leases can exhaust capacity even when
no messages are ready.
.TP
.B ELITE_RETIRED (3)
Generation no longer admits the requested operation.
.TP
.B ELITE_BUSY (4)
Calls, aliases, holders or object budgets prevent the requested transition.
.TP
.B ELITE_BAD_ABI (5), ELITE_BAD_LAYOUT (6), ELITE_BAD_IDENTITY (7)
The supplied format, geometry or identity is incompatible.
.TP
.B ELITE_NOT_READY (8)
Required lifecycle condition or terminal child evidence is not yet present.
.TP
.B ELITE_COUNTER_LIMIT (9)
A checked nonwrapping ticket or epoch ceiling prevents continuation.
.TP
.B ELITE_INTEGRITY (10)
An authorized metadata, phase or checksum check failed. Do not repair by
scanning or reinserting ordinary descriptor contents.
.TP
.B ELITE_OS_ERROR (11), ELITE_OUTCOME_UNCERTAIN (12)
OS failure or unresolved transfer outcome. Preserve any cleanup obligation.
.TP
.B ELITE_AUTHORITY_REQUIRED (13), ELITE_UNSUPPORTED (14)
The operation needs an authority or is not supported by the admitted profile.
.TP
.B ELITE_INVALID_LEASE (15), ELITE_INVALID_ARGUMENT (16)
The live local operation or argument failed validation. A magic check cannot
make an arbitrary dangling C pointer safe to pass.
.TP
.B ELITE_CREATE_CONFLICT (17)
Creation did not obtain an exclusive fresh name.
.SS Outcome codes
.TP
.B NONE (0)
No ownership transition specified by this result.
.TP
.B WRITE_OWNED (1), READ_OWNED (3)
The caller holds the returned write/read lease.
.TP
.B PUBLISHED (2), RETURNED (4)
Ownership transferred irrevocably through the relevant publication edge.
.TP
.B RETAINED (5), UNCERTAIN (6)
Responsibility remains or is unresolved. Neither means the block is free.
.PP
All outcome constants have the ELITE_ prefix. Use
.B elite_status_string
for diagnostics, not to derive ownership semantics from text.
.SH MEMORY ORDER AND PROGRESS
SPSC has one publication writer and one reclamation writer. Its polling
try path reserves privately, without a shared reservation CAS. Producer
release publication synchronizes with a covering consumer acquire; consumer
release reclamation synchronizes with a covering producer acquire before
reuse. Both edges are required.
.PP
NCQ-SC64 has two index queues: QF distributes free blocks and QR carries
completed blocks. Queue heads, tails and entry words use strong sequentially
consistent atomics. Entry installation is publication; tail advancement
follows and is helpable. Successful head CAS grants exclusive removal.
A losing CAS discards the entire dependent observation, not only expected.
FIFO means publication order, not reservation or consumer-completion order.
.PP
A live generation never wraps its counters or epochs. Near exhaustion it
retires instead of repeating an identity. Conditional queue lock-freedom
is system-wide progress, not per-caller fairness or a scheduling deadline.
True sharing, page faults, CPU preemption and finite-storage pressure remain.
.SH WAITING
.B elite_wait_data
and
.B elite_wait_space
are bounded scheduling hints for PARKABLE_SPSC. Retry the actual borrow or
reserve predicate afterward. A wake, interruption or timeout grants no
payload ownership. NCQ parking is unsupported. POLL_ONLY does not acquire
a hidden notification RMW cost.
.SH RETIREMENT, SIGNALS AND CRASHES
No library operation is promised async-signal-safe. A signal handler should
only set an appropriate flag or use a separately approved async-safe
notification; perform queue and management operations in normal execution.
SIGKILL runs no abort or finalizer.
.PP
.B elite_retire
or
.B elite_object_retire
closes new admission. Previously admitted operations may still finish.
.B elite_enable_drain
permits the implemented old-generation drain only in RETIRE_REQUESTED.
.B elite_object_quarantine
retains uncertain old storage; it does not revoke pointers.
.PP
A heartbeat timeout cannot distinguish a dead process from a resumable
writer. Do not steal its bytes. A failed holder may strand a token without
blocking other ready entries. Once all capacity is held, useful traffic
cannot continue. A missing sole SPSC role needs a new managed generation.
.PP
.B elite_authority_reap_child
observes registered owned-child termination. A stopped child is not fenced.
Normal cleanup uses
.B elite_detach
followed by
.B elite_object_ack_cleanup
at the authority. BUSY retains the connection. After QUARANTINED a read
return may be RETIRED/RETAINED; after alias closure,
.B elite_abandon_retained
ends local responsibility without reinserting the block.
.PP
.B elite_object_destroy
requires all issued grants resolved or fenced, not merely a zero attachment
count. The authority permits at most four objects and two quarantines.
.B elite_authority_destroy
likewise requires resolved responsibility. Safe replacement uses distinct
backing and does not redirect old virtual addresses onto successor storage.
.PP
There is no persistent recovery after power loss, automatic authority
takeover, exactly-once external transaction, or malicious-writer isolation.
.SH AUXILIARY INTERFACES
.B elite_object_info
and
.B elite_get_info
copy immutable cached metadata into a correctly aligned C11 object.
.B elite_binding_info
and
.B elite_local_layout
serve non-overlaid FFI introspection.
.B elite_validate_prefix
validates stable bytes; it must not inspect a concurrently rewritten live
header. The CRC helpers are
.BR elite_crc32 ,
.B elite_crc64
and
.BR elite_header_crc32 .
Header CRC coverage is the immutable 512-byte prefix with its CRC field
zeroed only in the calculation. CRC64 is optional and covers the valid
payload prefix. Neither authenticates a peer or repairs a data race.
.B elite_version_string
and
.B elite_version_number
report the native library release identity.
.SH EXAMPLES
Complete compiled examples are provided in examples/local_roundtrip.c,
examples/cpp_roundtrip.cpp and examples/python_roundtrip.py. The
live_throughput_demo executable uses two distinct POSIX rings and a spawned
responder. Its p50/p99 values are full instrumented RTT, never RTT/2.
